These five concepts are the building blocks of how the internet delivers content. Think of them as a hierarchy from smallest → largest:

Cache (a feature) → Edge Location (a site) → Region (a city) → AZ (a building)
                                              ↑
                        CDN (the network connecting edge locations)   

1. Cache
A cache is simply a temporary copy of data stored closer to the user so repeated requests don't have to go all the way back to the source. 

Cache hit → the copy exists and is fresh → served instantly
Cache miss → no copy, or it's expired → fetch from origin, store a copy, then serve 
Caches exist at every layer:

Layer	Example
Browser	Your Chrome stores images/CSS locally
CDN edge	A server in Mumbai stores a copy of image.jpg
Application	Redis/Memcached stores database query results
OS	Page cache holds recently read disk blocks

The core idea is the same everywhere: don't re-fetch what you already have.

2. CDN (Content Delivery Network)
A CDN is a globally distributed network of servers whose job is to serve content from a location close to the user instead of a single origin server. 

How it works, step by step:

User in Mumbai requests example.com/image.jpg
        │
        ▼
DNS resolves to nearest CDN edge (Mumbai PoP)
        │
        ▼
Edge checks its local cache
        │
        ├── HIT  → serves cached copy in ~5 ms  ✅
        │
        └── MISS → forwards to origin server (e.g., Virginia)
                   stores the response locally
                   serves it to the user          ⏱ ~200 ms   


Key CDN providers: Cloudflare, Akamai, Fastly, AWS CloudFront.

What a CDN caches:

Static assets (images, CSS, JS, videos) — always
HTML pages — if configured (full-page caching)
API responses — selectively, via Cache-Control headers
What controls caching:

TTL (Time-to-Live): how long a cached copy stays valid
Cache key: usually the URL + selected headers/cookies — determines what counts as the same object
Cache-Control headers: the origin tells the CDN max-age=3600 (cache for 1 hour) or no-cache (don't cache) 

. Edge Location (Point of Presence / PoP)
An edge location is a physical site where a CDN keeps its servers.  It's not where your application runs — it's a caching and delivery outpost placed in cities near end users. 

AWS has 600+ edge locations worldwide
Cloudflare has 300+ cities
They are smaller than full data centers — optimized for caching, not compute
Edge location ≠ Region. An edge location in Mumbai doesn't have your databases, your EC2 instances, or your Kubernetes clusters. It has caches and routing hardware.

Analogy: If your origin server is a warehouse in Virginia, an edge location is a delivery locker in Mumbai. You don't run your business from the locker — you just store a few popular items there so customers don't have to wait for shipping from Virginia.


4. Region
A Region is a geographic area where a cloud provider operates a full set of data centers and offers its complete service catalog (compute, storage, databases, networking, AI, etc.). 

AWS: 33+ regions (e.g., ap-south-1 = Mumbai, us-east-1 = Virginia)
Each region is independent — a failure in one doesn't affect another
You choose which region to deploy in based on:
Data residency laws (Indian data must stay in India)
Latency (closest to your users)
Cost (varies by region)
Service availability (not all services are in all regions)
Analogy: A Region is the city where you build your business. 



5. Availability Zone (AZ)
An Availability Zone is one or more physically separate data centers within a single Region, each with:

Independent power
Independent cooling
Independent networking
Redundant connectivity to the region backbone 
Typical region: 3–6 AZs, physically tens of kilometers apart (connected by low-latency fiber).

Why AZs matter:

If one AZ loses power, the others keep running → high availability
You spread instances across AZs so a single data center failure doesn't take down your app
Most cloud services (RDS Multi-AZ, ELB, EFS) are multi-AZ by default
Analogy: If a Region is the city, an AZ is one independent building in that city.  If one building catches fire, the others are fine.



